src/vendor/pdfio/ is the files listed in tools/pdfio-files.txt, copied from
the release tarball, with exactly the patches above applied.
tools/verify-vendor re-derives the tree from the pinned tarball and the
patch series and fails on any other difference. Each patch adds a
modification notice to every file it changes (Apache-2.0 section 4(b)).

  0001-visibility-override  _PDFIO_PUBLIC and _PDFIO_PRIVATE can be defined
                            on the command line. pdfio marks its API
                            visibility("default"), which would export it
                            from zupdf.so past $(C_VISIBILITY); Makevars
                            defines both empty, so only R_init_zupdf is
                            exported.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#183.
  0002-no-stdio             PDFIO_NO_STDIO leaves out the default error
                            callback's fprintf(stderr), ttf's errorf()
                            fallback to stderr and the three DEBUG value
                            printers, which R CMD check reports as
                            console output. zupdf always installs its own
                            callbacks, so none of these is reachable.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#184,
                            michaelrsweet/ttf#13.
  0003-date-buffer          The two date buffers grow from 32 to 80 bytes,
                            enough for any int the "D:%04d%02d..." format
                            can print. gcc's -Wformat-truncation, which R
                            CMD check reports, warned that 32 might not be.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#185.

  0004-undefined-behaviour  What UBSan reports in pdfio's own code: the
                            token callbacks, the crypt callbacks and the
                            dictionary and object-map comparators were
                            called through function pointers of another
                            type (clang's -fsanitize=function); they get
                            adapters or signatures of the right type. A
                            zero-length memcpy() from NULL in
                            pdfioStreamPeek() and pointer arithmetic on
                            NULL in the MD5 alignment test are guarded.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#186.

  0005-unsigned-shifts      Big-endian reads in ttf.c and pdfio's PNG reader
                            shifted a byte promoted to int left by 24, which
                            overflows when its high bit is set; UBSan
                            reported it reading a TrueType font. The bytes
                            are cast to unsigned first.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#187,
                            michaelrsweet/ttf#14.
  0010-ttf-callbacks        pdfio called its ttf error relay and ttfDelete()
                            through function pointers of another type; ASan
                            and UBSan in CI reported both at Stage 4. They
                            get signatures and an adapter of the right type.
                            (Numbered 0010 because 0006 to 0009 were already
                            taken by later stages' patches; patches apply in
                            numeric order and touch separate code.)
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#192.

  0006-dict-getstring       pdfioDictGetString() turned a binary value into a
                            string in place, so reading a UTF-16 text string
                            (as pdfio itself does for the XMP metadata at
                            close) made it be written back as raw UTF-8,
                            which other readers misread. It now returns the
                            converted string and leaves the value as it is.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#188.

  0007-number-casts         Numbers read from a file (/W, /Index, /Length,
                            /Prev, /N, filter parameters, the encryption
                            dictionary's /V, /R and /Length) were cast to
                            size_t, int or off_t unchecked, undefined for a
                            negative, NaN or huge value; the fuzzer found
                            /W. Clamping conversions in pdfio-private.h
                            replace the 23 casts; in-range values convert as
                            before.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#189.

  0008-repair-loop          repair_xref() skipped its line_offset update
                            when it could not read an object, so the next
                            object was read from a stale offset and the scan
                            could loop for ever on a damaged file; found by
                            fuzzing (a 716-byte file that never finished).
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#190.
  0009-index-cast           An /Index entry was cast to intmax_t unchecked,
                            which 0007 missed; fuzzing found a 1.9e104.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#191.
  0011-object-value-leak    An object whose own value is a hex string never
                            freed it, at close or when the repair scan read
                            the object again; LeakSanitizer reported it
                            fuzzing. The value is freed with the object and
                            before it is read again. Reported upstream as
                            michaelrsweet/pdfio#182.
  0012-trailer-value-leak   A trailer that was not a dictionary (a hex
                            string, say) was refused without being freed,
                            in load_xref() twice and repair_xref() once;
                            LeakSanitizer reported it fuzzing at Stage 7.
                            Reported upstream: michaelrsweet/pdfio#193.
  0013-objstm-value-leak    An object the repair scan had already read (a
                            hex string, say) was read again from an object
                            stream without its first value being freed;
                            LeakSanitizer reported it in the nightly fuzz
                            run. Upstream master keeps the first value but
                            leaks the copy it discards. Reported upstream:
                            michaelrsweet/pdfio#196.
  0014-date-utc             Without HAVE_TIMEGM, which pdfio's configure
                            sets and zupdf's portable Makevars cannot, a
                            date was converted with mktime(), in the local
                            time zone, and corrected by adding "timezone",
                            whose sign is the opposite: every date read was
                            off by twice the zone's offset outside UTC.
                            The fallback now counts days in the Gregorian
                            calendar. Reported upstream: pdfio#196.
  0015-copy-depth           Copying an object to another file follows its
                            references depth-first, through pdfioObjCopy(),
                            with no bound and a 32 KB buffer on the stack in
                            each frame: a page referring to a chain of 1,000
                            objects (a 54 KB file) overflowed the C stack.
                            _pdfioValueCopy() now counts its depth and
                            refuses past PDFIO_MAX_COPY_DEPTH (2,048), and
                            the buffer is on the heap. Found by review; to
                            be reported upstream.
  0016-object-limit         A compressed cross-reference stream can list
                            millions of objects from a few kilobytes, and
                            pdfio allocates each as it reads it: 90 KB
                            listing ten million took 1.2 GB before zupdf
                            could count them. _pdfioFileOpenMax() opens a
                            file with a limit, which add_obj() checks
                            ("Too many objects."); pdfioFileOpen() has none.
                            load_obj_stream() now stops when add_obj()
                            fails, where it used the NULL. Found by review.
                            Reported upstream: pdfio#196.
  0017-xref-shifts          A cross-reference stream's four-byte generation
                            was assembled with buffer[i] << 24 in int, and
                            its offset by shifting a signed intmax_t, both
                            undefined behaviour once the top bit is set;
                            UBSan reported the first fuzzing (0005 missed
                            it). Both are now built in unsigned types and
                            clamped. Reported upstream: pdfio#196.
  0018-number-checks        Two numbers read from a file that 0007 missed:
                            an indirect /Length was cast from double to
                            size_t unchecked (pdfioObjGetLength()), and a
                            cross-reference table's START COUNT pair was
                            counted past INTMAX_MAX. The nightly fuzz run
                            (UBSan) found both. The length is clamped with
                            _pdfioToSize() and an out-of-range pair sends
                            the file to repair. Reported upstream: #196.

None but 0006, 0014 and 0015 changes behaviour on well-formed input: 0006
only what is written, 0014 dates read outside UTC and 0015 copies that nest
deeper than 2,048 values.

